  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 136 AM EST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nFor the second half of the weekend and into early next week, the \npattern will begin to shift as the next low is expected to swing \nacross the Gulf as part of a larger longwave trough. Flow will shift \nmuch more southwesterly as this low advects across the Gulf, which \nwill enhance moisture advection back into South Florida. Latest \nguidance suggests PWATs rising above average to the 1.8-2.0\" range \nwith vorticity advection occurring around the mid-level trough into \nthe region. However, vertical growth could be tempered by an upper \nlevel anticyclone centered over the Caribbean that will be \ncirculating over South Florida at the same time as the vorticity and \nmoisture advection occurs around the base of the mid-level trough.  \n\nAs a result of the counteracting features mentioned above, this \nsetup may not produce steady rainfall until Tuesday when the \nanticyclone gets forced out of the area by the incoming mid-level \ntrough. Thus, at the moment the expectation is for Sunday and Monday \nto experience some scattered shower activity with perhaps an outside \nchance for a thunderstorm, but this would be a highly conditional \nrisk given the expected vertical growth capping caused by the upper \nlevel high. Currently, Tuesday has the best chance for a more \nwidespread rainfall day along with periods of moderate to heavy \nrain. Uncertainty still exists for specific impacts, but we will \ncontinue to monitor. The next frontal boundary is then currently \nexpected to clear out the deeper moisture sometime towards the \nmiddle to late portion of next week, although the strength of this \nfront may not result in moisture being fully cleared out. \nNevertheless, rain chances are expected to drop considerable by by \nthe middle to end of next week.\n\nTemperatures will rise back into the upper 70s to low 80s each day \nthrough the long term period, although some areas may reach the mid \n80s for the middle of next week. Overnight lows also increase back \nup, reaching the upper 60s to low 70s.",
